description:
The jcl-bridge rejects any job containing `DCB=dsname` or `DCB=(dsname,subparm,...)`, the model-dataset form of DCB. The whole job is lost: no Job JSON, so cobble gets no nodes for it and jackal cannot run it.

Reach: in the census corpus this form is the GDG idiom `DSN=X(+1),DISP=(NEW,CATLG),DCB=(<gdg-base>,RECFM=..,LRECL=..)` on >=25% of jobs (212+ files, a lower bound). So at least a quarter of a comparable corpus fails to parse before any analysis. Highest priority in the static track; every other item is moot for a job that does not parse.

Reproduce:
  //J1 JOB (A),'D',CLASS=A
  //S1 EXEC PGM=SORT
  //O DD DSN=P.X,DISP=(NEW,CATLG),DCB=(P.MODEL,RECFM=FB,LRECL=80)
-> `line 3:43 no viable alternative at input ...DCB=(P.MODEL`. `DCB=P.MODEL` fails the same way at 3:42. `DCB=(RECFM=FB,LRECL=80)` and `DCB=*.SORTIN` parse.

Likely cause: JCLParser.g4:411 already lists `datasetName` as an alternative of `ddParmDCB_Parameter`, so the rule exists. The lexer is probably in its DCB mode after `DCB=` and does not emit the token types `datasetName` expects. Confirm against the lexer modes before changing anything; do not add a second rule.

Fix in jackal/jcl-bridge (the grammar copy forge owns, not vendor/). Add a Java test beside DcbContinuationTest/NullDcbSubparameterTest, and flip the S0 fixture case. Run `make jar-force` (see jackal-231: `make jar` does not rebuild on Java-only changes).

Out of scope: carrying DCB attributes into the model (jackal-1o2.12). The parse only has to succeed; the serializer may keep ignoring DCB for now.

notes:
INCOMPLETE AS PORTED — cobble validated this against graph building only; it never lowers to IR.

red-dragon's condition_lowering.py has two silent fallbacks for an unrecognised ExprNode:
  logger.warning('Unknown expression node type: %s', ...); return ctx.const_to_reg(0)
and in _expr_node_to_arg_dict:
  return {'kind': 'ref', 'name': getattr(node, 'name', '')}

So adding FigurativeNode/LengthOfNode/DfhRespNode to make the ASG parse, without teaching the lowering what they mean, trades a loud ValueError for silently wrong IR: a figurative constant lowers to 0 (accidentally correct for ZEROS, wrong for SPACES, HIGH-VALUES, LOW-VALUES and ALL literals), and LENGTH OF becomes a reference to a field named ''.

Definition of done therefore includes the lowering, not just the parse:
- figurative constants lower to the right fill value per receiving item (SPACES, HIGH-VALUES, LOW-VALUES, ZEROS, ALL <lit>), not to 0;
- LENGTH OF lowers to the compile-time byte count;
- DFHRESP resolves through the existing dfhresp prepass rather than becoming a field ref;
- an ExprNode the lowering does not handle should fail loudly rather than returning const 0 — the current fallback is what makes this class of bug silent.

Tests must cover a lowered/executed path, not only expr_from_dict round-tripping.

description:
A DD that cannot be allocated is a Python exception today, so the job result is lost. z/OS fails the step before its program starts. Blueprint §6 gives it the outcome `AllocationFailed(ddname, error)`, one of:

- DATASET_NOT_FOUND: `//IN DD DSN=NO.SUCH,DISP=SHR`, no such data set catalogued;
- DUPLICATE_NAME: `//OUT DD DSN=PROD.TRANS,DISP=(NEW,CATLG)` when PROD.TRANS already exists;
- TEMP_NOT_CREATED: `//IN DD DSN=&&X,DISP=OLD` with no earlier NEW (today `test_missing_temp_raises` pins the exception).

The job outcome becomes `JobAllocationFailed(step, failure)`. Needs the step lifecycle (red-dragon-forge-lrk), which owns allocation, and the outcome type (red-dragon-forge-5fu). Overlaps red-dragon-forge-93f on DUPLICATE_NAME once the catalog is persistent.

The jcl-bridge rejects the whole job when the JOB statement's only positional parameter is a quoted string and the statement continues on the next line.

Reproduce:
//J1 JOB 'EXTRACT TRAN',
//  CLASS=A
//S1 EXEC PGM=X
-> line 1:23 mismatched input ',\n' expecting {<EOF>, DELIMITER_STATEMENT, ...

Parses: the same card on one line (`//J1 JOB 'EXTRACT TRAN',CLASS=A`), and the continued card with an accounting field first (`//J1 JOB (A),'EXTRACT TRAN',`).

Seen in one CardDemo job (app-transaction-type-db2). Fix in jackal/jcl-bridge with a Java test beside JclSerializerTest; the CardDemo snapshot (object-model build, unit 1) records it as a parse error until then.
